Q: Using std::start_lifetime_as multiple times with the same source object A: <p>First question, yes because <code>std::byte</code> arrays are providing storage.
Second question, I'd say that <code>start_lifetime_as</code> is not for this use-case but it seems legit though (more for creating C++ object in C buffer from C-API). Intent could be cleaner with <code>std::construct_at</code> or placement <code>new</code>.</p>
<p><code>std::bit_cast</code> is just as OK but you could replace it by a <code>std::memcpy</code> to restore the bits after placement <code>new</code> (while <code>start_lifetime_as</code> is guaranteed to leave the bits untouched).</p>
<p>You can remove the <code>std::array</code> and replace it by a plain array (and it will be less debatable, regarding the alignement of <code>storage.data()</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>include &lt;cstddef&gt;
#include &lt;cstdint&gt;
#include &lt;cstring&gt;
#include &lt;iomanip&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;

using DoubleParts = std::tuple&lt;std::uint16_t, std::uint16_t, std::uint32_t&gt;;

DoubleParts lifetimeBits(const double value) noexcept {
    static_assert(sizeof(double) == sizeof(std::uint16_t) +
                                        sizeof(std::uint16_t) +
                                        sizeof(std::uint32_t));

    // Copy the object representation into aligned byte storage. The byte array
    // provides storage for the three implicit-lifetime integer objects.
    alignas(DoubleParts) std::byte storage[sizeof(double)];

    // Start three independent object lifetimes in non-overlapping regions.
    // actually new is useless there because these are implicit lifetime objects.
    const auto* first = new(static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(storage)) std::uint16_t;
    const auto* second = new(static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(storage+sizeof(std::uint16_t))) std::uint16_t;
    const auto* third = new(static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(storage+2*sizeof(std::uint16_t))) std::uint32_t;

    // set the bits
    std::memcpy(static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(storage),static_cast&lt;void const *&gt;(&amp;value),sizeof(double));

    // Copy the values into the tuple before the local storage is destroyed.
    return {*first, *second, *third};
}

int main() {
    constexpr double value = 1.0;
    const auto [first, second, third] = lifetimeBits(value);

    // The values reflect the platform's byte order. They are not numeric
    // conversions from double to integer types.
    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; &quot;first:  0x&quot; &lt;&lt; first &lt;&lt; '\n'
              &lt;&lt; &quot;second: 0x&quot; &lt;&lt; second &lt;&lt; '\n'
              &lt;&lt; &quot;third:  0x&quot; &lt;&lt; third &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/Y8bWeb6qT" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LIVE</a></p>
<p>Yet I think it answers the question in the text but not in the spirit. What you'd like maybe to remove the intermediate storage?
It will be probably removed altogether by the compiler and its optimization.</p>

Q: How to get the last insert id of a specific table in MySQL A: <p>You can use <a href="http://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.0/en/information-functions.html#function_last-insert-id" rel="nofollow"><strong>LAST_INSERT_ID()</strong></a> function.</p>

<pre><code>INSERT INTO blahblah (test1, test 2) VALUES ('test1', 'test2');
    //this query will return id. Save it in one variable

 select LAST_INSERT_ID()
</code></pre>

<p>In short, save the last insert id in one variable and then use it in blahblah3</p>
